Sample 1:
The lightning to the west threw his face into stark relief.  The long scar from cheek to jaw glowing bone-white for half a second before the dark swallowed it again. "Her husband is six months in the cold ground. Six. And you arrived three weeks ago with your foreign trinkets and your honeyed counsel, and now you presume to—"
"To what?" Vael's smile was small as he leaned back, crossing his arms casually across his broadened chest. "To love her? To listen to her? To sit beside her in the long hours when even her own captain of the guard cannot find a single word that does not taste of duty?"
Thunder rolled across the clouds, deafening their breaths as fat drops of rain splattered against their dulled armor. "Do not mistake her loneliness for affection. She is queen-regent of three provinces, and you are a wandering hedge-mage with a silver tongue and an empty title. When she wakes from this fog"
"When she wakes." Vael's loud voice overrode the rising wind. He pushed off the merlon and stepped fully into the falling rain, letting it darken his pale hair to the color of wet straw. "That is the difference between us, captain. You believe she is sleeping. I believe she has been awake for some time, and is simply tired of pretending otherwise around men who only ever saw her as her late husband's widow."
The captain's hand had drifted to the pommel of his longsword. The leather wrap was dark now, soaked through, the brass crossguard shining like a coin under the wash of the tower lamps. "Choose your next words with great care, mage."
"I have already chosen them." Vael did not look to the sword, instead casting his gaze to the lone figure below.  She was sheltered from the rain in a cozy alcove lit by a flickering brazier. "And so, I suspect, has she. The question, Sir Corwin, is whether you will keep standing between her and the only door she has chosen for herself, or whether you will finally, mercifully, step aside."
Sample 2:
Vivienne brought a crystal flute to her lips, the pale wine catching the chandelier's light. "Look at him. He's been dancing for three hours without a single misstep. His stamina on the floor is positively marvelous." 
Seraphina snapped her painted peacock fan open, her ruby bodice glittering as her shoulders shook with mirth. "It's not just his feet that possess such precision, darling. Did you see the way he managed the eastern trade delegations this morning? The man has an exquisite eye for detail." 
"He had better keep that eye sharp." Genevieve leaned against the cold marble balustrade, her sapphire gown pooling around her feet. She took a measured sip from her drink. "Because according to the lunar calendar, tomorrow night marks the beginning of the solstice festival. Which means, by our sacred contract, his schedule belongs entirely to me for the next three days." 
Vivienne nudged Genevieve's elbow, sending the white wine dancing to the rim of her glass. "Oh, don't be greedy, Gen. You had him for the entirety of the spring equinox. I believe he spent so much time tending to your 'private gardens' that he nearly forgot what the rest of the estate looked like." 
"A queen must ensure her lands are thoroughly tilled." Genevieve favored her fellow wife with a slow wink of her purple eye. "But fear not. I have every intention of returning him to you in pristine condition by Friday. Though, he might require a bit of pampering." 
Seraphina snapped her fan closed, casting her blue eyes between the two of them. "Well, if we are discussing the division of labor, I must remind you both that the Grand Gala is next week. I require his full, undivided attention for the hosting duties. He needs to look radiant, rested, and entirely devoted to the family's prestige." 
"He manages all three of us with absolute grace, Phina." Vivienne's breath hitched as their husband caught her eye through the crowd, offering a brief smile showing the whites of his eyes. "The man is a treasure. Honestly, if we didn't share the burden of his affections, we'd probably wear him down to the bone within a month." 
"True," said Genevieve, raising her thin crystal glass into the candlelight. "It would be a terrible waste of a perfectly good husband to keep him all to oneself." Seraphina smiled, clinking her flute against the others. "To a well-regulated household, then. May our lord never run out of energy, and may his devotion to his wives remain perfectly divided."
Sample 3:
Mina slid the earthenware mug across the scarred oak table, the amber ale catching the hearth-light as its heavy, cream-colored foam sloshed over the rim. "Drink up. You're staring at the blacksmith's apprentice so hard you're liable to melt his anvil from across the room." 
Elena caught the mug, her cheeks flushed a warm rose, laughing as she took a long draught. "Can you blame me? Have you seen the way he handles a heavy hammer? The sheer... endurance it must take to keep that rhythm all day long." 
"Oh, I've noticed." Mina smiled and leaned back into the smoky blue shadows of their booth, tracing the rim of her own clay mug. She cast a long look through the golden haze of the tavern, taking in his compact frame and musculature, his skin gleaming bronze in the distant, pulsing orange glow of the forge. 
"He's got an incredibly sturdy frame. Built for heavy lifting, definitely. I imagine he doesn't tire too easily." 
"Exactly!" Elena leaned in, her eyes glinting in the low light as she whispered, the warm, yeasty smell of ale emanating from her lips. "The man is an absolute treasure. Honestly, Mina, if he ever offers to stoke your furnace, you'd be a fool to say no." 
"Please, if he brings that kind of heat to my hearth, I'm letting him move the logs around however he sees fit," Mina chuckled, raising her mug in a mock toast, the wet clay catching a sudden flare of crimson light from the fire. "There's more than enough of him to go around, anyway. It'd be a crime to keep a craftsman that talented all to oneself." 
Elena clinked her mug against Mina's, her grin widening as droplets of ale spilled onto the darkened, bruised-purple grain of the old wood. "To shared appreciation of fine workmanship, then. May his iron never lose its temper."
Sample 4:
"Drink something, Isolde. You've been staring at that garden for hours, and the chamomile's gone tepid twice already."
Isolde's fingers tightened in her lap.  Rays of sunlight fell across her chestnut hair, unpinned and falling in loose waves, glowed russet where the light struck.
"She loved this hour of the day. Beatrice. She used to say the afternoon light was the only honest light, because it had nowhere left to hide." A small, wet laugh escaped her, and her hazel eyes lifted to his, rimmed pink and bright with unshed tears. "She loved you, too, Rowan. I knew it. I think you knew it. And I— I am a wretched creature for sitting here and still being grateful that I'm the one you came to find."
Rowan exhaled slowly, the warm column of air visible for an instant where it passed through the sunbeam. He reached out and folded one of her cold hands between his own, his palms broad and sun-browned against her pale palms.
"You're not wretched. You're left behind, which is a harder country to live in than people give it credit for." His thumb traced a slow arc across her knuckles. "Beatrice knew what I felt. She knew before I did, I think. She told me once, out by the pear orchard, that loving someone meant wanting their happiness more than your own version of it. I didn't understand her then. I'm beginning to."
Isolde's breath caught audibly, her shoulders trembling beneath the cream linen of her mourning shawl as a fresh tear traced down and fell, darkening the pale fabric of her skirt. She turned her hand inside his so that their palms lay flush, fingers threading together in the warm honeyed light.
"Then sit with me. Just…sit. Don't say anything kind yet, because I'll come apart if you do. Just let the afternoon be honest with us for a little while."
